mixin graphCard(vertices, edges, label)
  .forbidden-card
    svg(viewBox="0 0 160 72" aria-label=label role="img")
      each edge in edges
        line(x1=vertices[edge[0]][0] y1=vertices[edge[0]][1] x2=vertices[edge[1]][0] y2=vertices[edge[1]][1])
      each vertex in vertices
        circle(cx=vertex[0] cy=vertex[1] r="5")
    small= label

section
  section.eighty-percent
    h3 Why second eigenvalue multiplicity?
    p
      | An equiangular line system gives a graph $G$ with
      br
      | $M=(1-\alpha)I-2\alpha A(G)+\alpha J\succeq0$ and $\operatorname{rank}M\le d$.
    p.fragment Set $\lambda=\frac{1-\alpha}{2\alpha}$, so $M=\alpha\bigl(2(\lambda I-A(G))+J\bigr)$.
    p.fragment
      | Rank-nullity gives
      br
      | $|G|\le d+\operatorname{mult}_G(\lambda)+1$.
    p.fragment.red Large exceptional line systems require large multiplicity of a non-leading eigenvalue.

  section.eighty-percent
    h3 Two competing constructions
    .construction-race
      .construction-card.generic
        h4 Generic
        p $G=G_1\sqcup\cdots\sqcup G_t$
        p $\rho(G_i)\le\lambda$
        p.fragment Repeating a smallest graph with $\rho=\lambda$ gives linear growth.
      .construction-card.exceptional
        h4 Exceptional
        p $\lambda_1(G)>\lambda_2(G)=\lambda$
        p.fragment Size is powered by the multiplicity of the second eigenvalue.
        p.fragment Control that multiplicity, and the generic construction eventually wins.

  section.eighty-percent
    h3 The case $\lambda=\sqrt2$
    p $\alpha^*=\frac{1}{1+2\sqrt2}$ and $k(\sqrt2)=3$.
    .cherry-row.fragment
      - for (var i = 0; i < 6; i++)
        svg(height="70" width="90" viewBox="0 0 90 70")
          line(x1="10" y1="55" x2="45" y2="12")
          line(x1="45" y1="12" x2="80" y2="55")
          circle(cx="10" cy="55" r="5")
          circle(cx="45" cy="12" r="5")
          circle(cx="80" cy="55" r="5")
    p.fragment
      | Disjoint cherries give
      br
      | $\left\lfloor\frac{3(d-1)}2\right\rfloor$ equiangular lines in $\mathbb R^d$.
    p.fragment To prove this is eventually optimal, it would suffice to show that exceptional constructions are $d+o(d)$.

