    p.fragment.red The exceptional branch does not merely lose the race—it stops.

section
  section.eighty-percent
    h3 Seidel matrix and switching
    p $S(G)=J-I-2A(G)$
    p.fragment
      | $n$ equiangular lines with angle $\arccos\alpha$ in $\mathbb R^d$
      br
      | $\Longleftrightarrow I+\alpha S(G)\succeq0$ and $\operatorname{rank}(I+\alpha S(G))\le d$.
    .switching-box.fragment
      p Switch a vertex set $U$: toggle every edge across $U\mid V(G)\setminus U$.
      p $S(G^U)=D_U S(G)D_U$
    p.fragment Switching preserves the Seidel spectrum and the line system up to orthogonal transformations.

  section.eighty-percent
    h3 The generic family
    p If $G$ is a disjoint union of isolated vertices, edges, and cherries, then $\rho(G)\le\sqrt2$ and
    p.fragment $$S(G)+\frac1{\alpha^*}I=J+2\bigl(\sqrt2 I-A(G)\bigr)\succeq0.$$
    p.fragment If $c$ components are cherries, then
    p.fragment $$\operatorname{null}\left(S(G)+\frac1{\alpha^*}I\right)\le c-1.$$
    p.fragment Since $c\le |G|/3$, we get $|G|\le\max\left(d,\left\lfloor\frac{3(d-1)}2\right\rfloor\right)$.

  section.eighty-percent
    h3 Five forbidden seeds
    p Every graph outside $\langle P_3\rangle_\pm$ contains, up to switching, one of these five induced subgraphs.
    .forbidden-grid.fragment
      +graphCard([[15,36],[50,36],[85,36],[120,36],[150,36]], [[0,1],[1,2],[2,3]], "5 vertices")
      +graphCard([[25,15],[25,57],[62,36],[112,36],[147,36]], [[0,1],[1,2],[2,0],[3,4]], "5 vertices")
      +graphCard([[20,15],[20,57],[55,36],[90,36],[125,36],[155,36]], [[0,2],[1,2],[2,3],[4,5]], "6 vertices")
      +graphCard([[12,15],[12,57],[47,36],[92,36],[127,15],[127,57]], [[0,2],[1,2],[2,3],[3,4],[3,5]], "6 vertices")
      +graphCard([[15,15],[15,57],[50,36],[85,36],[108,36],[131,36],[154,36]], [[0,2],[1,2],[2,3]], "7 vertices")
    p.fragment A minimal forbidden graph has at most 8 vertices, so this list is finite and checkable.

  section.eighty-percent
    h3 Enumerate every exception
    .proof-flow
      .proof-step
        span 1
        p Start from the five forbidden seeds
      .proof-arrow →
      .proof-step
        span 2
        p Add one vertex with every possible neighborhood
      .proof-arrow →
      .proof-step
        span 3
        p Keep $S(G)+I/\alpha^*\succeq0$
      .proof-arrow →
      .proof-step
        span 4
        p Deduplicate up to switching
    p.fragment Repeat by order. The list at order 29 is empty.
    p.fragment
      | Positive semidefiniteness and rank are checked exactly in $\mathbb Z[\sqrt2]$;
      br
      | code and complete output accompany the paper.

section
  section.eighty-percent
    h3 What we needed versus what we found
    .surprise-grid
      .surprise-card.needed
        h4 Needed for asymptotics
        p Bound second-eigenvalue multiplicity
        p $\Downarrow$
        p Exceptional constructions are eventually outperformed
      .surprise-card.found.fragment
        h4 What classification revealed
        p No exceptional graph has order $>28$
        p $\Downarrow$
        p.red Exceptional constructions are absolutely bounded
    p.fragment The stronger phenomenon was not predicted by the asymptotic theory.

  section.eighty-percent
    h3 Open problem: when are exceptions finite?
    p For general $\alpha$, let $\lambda=(1-\alpha)/(2\alpha)$ and let $\mathcal G(\lambda)$ be the connected graphs with spectral radius at most $\lambda$.
    .conjecture.fragment
      p Problem from the paper
      p
        | For which $\alpha\in(0,1)$ are there only finitely many graphs outside
        br
        | $\langle\mathcal G(\lambda)\rangle_\pm$ with smallest Seidel eigenvalue at least $-1/\alpha$?
    ul
      li.fragment Yes for $\alpha=\alpha^*$: $63{,}673$ exceptions, all of order at most 28.
      li.fragment The same method gives 902 exceptions for $\alpha=1/3$, again all of order at most 28.
      li.fragment No for certain explicitly constructed angles where $N_\alpha(d)\ge d+\Omega(\log\log d)$.

  section.eighty-percent(data-background-color="#121426")
    h3(style="color:white") The unexpected conclusion
    p(style="color:white")
      | Generic constructions do not merely win eventually.
      br
      span(style="color:#ff9fae") At $\alpha^*$, every exceptional construction is bounded in order.
    p.fragment(style="color:white") Maximum exceptional order: $28$.

